Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "teammate DRIVE preceding a PNR",
    "sequential possession sequence",
    "points per possession (PPP)",
    "play-type labeling",
    "DRIVE->PNR transition"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "measurement reliability of play-type labels for DRIVE and PNR",
    "temporal sequence definition for same-team offensive possessions",
    "confounding by game state, lineup, and opponent response",
    "sample size for DRIVE->PNR sequences with different initiators",
    "placebo dependence and multiplicity in sequential premium analysis"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF07",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "After the opponent runs play type X, the next offensive play type Y scores above Y's unconditional H2 baseline, and every PT has a defensive aftershock. A2 aftershock 8/8 PTs replicate all-games and playoffs (CUT +0.119 n=21,398 / PO +0.131 n=1,145 through TRANSITION +0.135 n=26,262 / +0.126 n=1,529). Top pair premiums: PUTBACK->TRANSITION +0.084 n=565; CUT->PULL_UP +0.065 n=2,620."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF40",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Specific same-player and teammate X->Y sequences carry PPP premiums above Y's baseline. Top same-player: P. George DRIVE->SPOT_UP +0.560 n=31 t=+2.2; L. Doncic DRIVE->PNR +0.309 n=53. Top teammate: J. Brown->J. Tatum DRIVE->PNR +0.326 n=43. Bottom: D. Bane DRIVE->PNR -0.324 n=70 t=-2.7. Most cells DIRECTIONAL (n<50). Later Brown->Tatum +0.326 was shown to be a placebo artifact (APPS-070)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Play-type PPP differences are real efficiency, not labeling/foul artifacts. OTHER: 36% end in fouls, PPP inflated (FOUL_BUCKET). DRIVE: 12pp below foul benchmark (FOUL_LEAK). PNR shows 22% foul rate. Only 335 of 50,668 possessions (0.7%) map to a real play type via initiation_type."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-058",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "PBP-derived shot-level PNR rates sit in a realistic band and vary by team. 2024-25 shot-level PNR share of half-court shots ranges from OKC 7.567% to IND 13.824%. Rates are a calibration input for PBPProxyPNR (later logs show calibrated PNR ~17.9-19.6% of possessions)."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF40",
      "what_failed": "Descriptive exploration of player and teammate sequential premiums, including DRIVE->PNR",
      "why": "small sample (many cells n<50), no multiplicity correction, mixed signs, and later a prominent teammate DRIVE->PNR case (Brown->Tatum +0.326) was identified as a placebo artifact"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
      "what_failed": "Audit of play-type labeling for DRIVE and PNR",
      "why": "DRIVE PPP is 12pp below a foul benchmark due to foul leakage; initiation_type maps only 0.7% of possessions, indicating severe classification errors that would bias any DRIVE->PNR PPP analysis"
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "SF40 directly investigated teammate DRIVE->PNR sequences and found no consistent PPP premium; some positive cases were later artifacts and small samples preclude reliable inference.",
    "SF-CHT-025 demonstrates that play-type labels for DRIVE and PNR suffer from systematic errors (foul leakage, low initiation_type mapping) that would distort PPP comparisons.",
    "SF07 shows that sequential premiums exist on opponent-offense pairs, but the defensive aftershock pattern does not directly address teammate-offense sequences."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "whether a real causal effect of a preceding teammate DRIVE on PNR PPP exists after correcting for labeling bias and confounding",
    "the true sample size of DRIVE->PNR sequences with distinct initiators across multiple seasons",
    "the magnitude of confounding from game context, lineup, and opponent adjustments that could mimic a premium"
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The combination of small expected sample sizes (especially requiring different initiators), known labeling artifacts for DRIVE and PNR, and the previous failure to find robust premiums in a closely related analysis strongly suggests that a simple hypothesis test of the broad question will be underpowered and highly susceptible to artifacts.",
    "Even a well-designed study would first require substantial improvements in play-type classification reliability and an explicit strategy to handle confounding, neither of which is part of the posed question."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "exact number of same-team DRIVE->PNR transitions in the available data",
    "whether improved labels could rescue the analysis",
    "whether a consistent effect exists for some subset of teams or players but is averaged out in the full sample"
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "Without the historical evidence, the question might have appeared testable by simply comparing PPP of PNR possessions preceded or not preceded by a teammate DRIVE. The evidence shows that a nearly identical analysis (SF40) was attempted and produced fragile, small-sample results that were later partly attributed to a placebo artifact, and a concurrent labeling audit (SF-CHT-025) reveals systematic errors in DRIVE and PNR labels that would invalidate a PPP comparison. These findings convert an initially plausible question into one with a high risk of producing a spurious artifact."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.12,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
        "text": "Play-type labels for DRIVE and PNR are known to suffer from foul leakage and low initiation_type mapping (SF-CHT-025), biasing PPP measurements."
      },
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Teammate DRIVE->PNR sequences with different initiators are likely rare; SF40 found most cells had n<50 even when pooling multiple seasons, and the generic question would still require enough observations to detect a plausible premium."
      },
      {
        "code": "PLACEBO_DEPENDENCE",
        "text": "SF40 found that a prominent positive case (Brown->Tatum DRIVE->PNR) was later identified as a placebo artifact, indicating that uncorrected sequential premiums can reflect noise rather than signal."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF40",
      "FAM:SF-CHT-025"
    ],
    "summary": "SF40 explored teammate DRIVE->PNR PPP premiums descriptively and found mixed, small-sample results, later partly retracted as placebo artifacts. SF-CHT-025 exposed fundamental labeling problems that would bias any DRIVE->PNR test. The core empirical question has therefore been informally examined with discouraging outcomes, but no formal, adequately corrected hypothesis test has been performed."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
      "text": "DRIVE and PNR play-type labels are error-prone (foul leakage, low initiation_type mapping) leading to misclassification of the exposure and outcome."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "The number of qualifying sequences (teammate DRIVE immediately preceding PNR with a different initiator) is likely small, reducing power and inflating the risk of false positives."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Game state (score margin, clock), lineup quality, opponent defensive disruption, and pace may correlate with both a DRIVE occurrence and subsequent PNR success."
    },
    {
      "code": "PLACEBO_DEPENDENCE",
      "text": "Sequential premiums found in exploratory analyses can be artifacts of noise, as shown by the Brown->Tatum DRIVE->PNR retraction."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Although the question appears singular, any exploratory search across players or subsets would introduce multiplicity."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.05,
    "rejected": 0.05,
    "untestable": 0.2,
    "artifact": 0.5,
    "uncertain": 0.2
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The difference in points per possession (PPP) for PNR possessions that are immediately preceded (on the next same-team offensive trip) by a DRIVE possession initiated by a different teammate, compared to PNR possessions not so preceded, controlling for game and possession covariates.",
    "unit": "A same-team offensive possession pair (t, t+1 in same-team sequence), where possession t is DRIVE and possession t+1 is PNR with a different initiator.",
    "sample": "All such possession pairs from NBA regular season and playoff games in 2022-23 through 2025-26, excluding garbage time (Q4/OT with margin ≥ 20 pts) and possessions with ambiguous play-type labels (e.g., those failing high-confidence classification thresholds).",
    "power": "Using a two-sample t-test with unequal variances, with alpha = 0.05 (one-sided), a sample of ~1,500 DRIVE→PNR pairs would provide 80% power to detect a PPP difference of ~0.08, which is plausible based on SF40’s scattered observations. The actual sample size is unknown but expected to be borderline, making the test underpowered for small effects.",
    "controls": "Linear regression adjustment for: score margin at start of the DRIVE possession, period, seconds remaining in period, home/away, rest days, five-man lineup strength (SRS or net rating), indicator for whether the DRIVE resulted in a made basket or defensive rebound vs. other end-of-possession events, and opponent defensive rating in the prior 10 games.",
    "falsification": "1) Test the same premium for DRIVE→play-type pairs other than PNR (e.g., DRIVE→SPOT_UP, DRIVE→ISO) to see if any outperformance is generic. 2) Use a pseudo-predecessor placebo: compare PNR PPP when the preceding same-team possession was two possessions ago and the intervening possession was not a DRIVE, expecting no premium. 3) Check symmetry by examining whether a preceding PNR raises DRIVE PPP.",
    "multiplicity": "The analysis tests a single pre-registered contrast (DRIVE→PNR vs. no-DRIVE). No multiplicity correction is required, but all checks listed under falsification are reported with unadjusted p-values and interpreted qualitatively.",
    "decision_rule": "Reject the null (no premium) if the one-sided p-value from the regression-adjusted model is < 0.05 and the point estimate is positive with a magnitude ≥ 0.03 PPP, with cluster-robust standard errors at the team×season level. A finding is considered spurious if any falsification test is significant in the same direction or if sensitivity analyses removing low-confidence labels halve the estimate.",
    "data_version": "Graph database of play-type possession records built from NBA stats API play-by-play, as of 2026-06-14."
  }
}

